---
// Why louder sounds fuller: equal-loudness contours. At low volume you need much more bass & treble to
// hear them as loud, so turning up brings the extremes forward. Static teaching diagram for the
// level-matching article. Simplified/illustrative, not measured contours.
---

<figure class="fig">
  <div class="figscroll"><svg viewBox="0 0 760 320" role="img" aria-label="Two equal-loudness curves. The low-volume curve rises steeply at the bass and treble ends; the high-volume curve is flatter, showing that raising volume brings bass and treble forward.">
    <!-- plot frame -->
    <rect x="70" y="30" width="640" height="238" fill="none" stroke="#34383d" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
    <!-- freq axis -->
    <g font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="10" fill="#565b60" text-anchor="middle">
      <text x="70" y="286">20</text>
      <text x="230" y="286">100</text>
      <text x="390" y="286">1k</text>
      <text x="550" y="286">5k</text>
      <text x="710" y="286">20k</text>
      <text x="390" y="304" fill="#868d92">frequency &#8594;</text>
    </g>
    <text x="52" y="150" text-anchor="middle" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="10" fill="#868d92" transform="rotate(-90 52 150)">level to sound equally loud</text>

    <!-- low-volume contour: deep U (needs much more bass + treble) -->
    <path d="M70 60 C170 150 260 214 390 220 C520 214 620 128 710 78" fill="none" stroke="#FF9E2C" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-dasharray="6 5" opacity="0.8"></path>
    <!-- high-volume contour: flatter -->
    <path d="M70 150 C190 210 300 224 390 222 C500 224 610 196 710 168" fill="none" stroke="#FF9E2C" stroke-width="2.6"></path>

    <!-- labels for the two curves -->
    <text x="150" y="96" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" fill="#FF9E2C">at low volume</text>
    <text x="470" y="205" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="11" fill="#ffb257">at high volume</text>

    <!-- takeaway arrows at the extremes -->
    <g stroke="#565b60" stroke-width="1.4" fill="#565b60" font-family="var(--mono)" font-size="10">
      <line x1="96" y1="140" x2="96" y2="70" marker-end="url(#el-up)"></line>
      <line x1="686" y1="158" x2="686" y2="90" marker-end="url(#el-up)"></line>
      <text x="104" y="112">more bass</text>
      <text x="678" y="130" text-anchor="end">more air</text>
    </g>
    <defs>
      <marker id="el-up" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="5" refY="2" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto">
        <path d="M0 8 L5 0 L10 8 z" fill="#565b60"></path>
      </marker>
    </defs>
  </svg></div>
  <figcaption>
    Your ears hear bass and treble relatively more as volume rises &mdash; so the louder of two things sounds
    fuller and &ldquo;better,&rdquo; for reasons that aren&rsquo;t about quality. Match levels, or you&rsquo;re
    just hearing volume.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .fig {
    margin: 28px 0 0;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: 18px 18px 14px;
  }
  .fig svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    /* These diagrams are drawn on a fixed 760-unit grid, so scaling the SVG to a narrow column
       scales the labels with it: at a 342px article column a 10px label lands near 4.5 CSS px.
       Hold a legible floor and let the figure scroll sideways instead of shrinking the type. */
    min-width: 600px;
  }
  .fig .figscroll {
    overflow-x: auto;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
    -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch;
  }
  @media (min-width: 700px) {
    .fig svg { min-width: 0; }
    .fig .figscroll { overflow-x: visible; }
  }
  .fig figcaption {
    margin-top: 12px;
    font-size: 13px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--muted-2);
    font-family: var(--mono);
  }
</style>
